-- title:
--   Edge transport fixing or reversing the ends of an edge
-- statement:
--   Let $r$ be a prime, $\mathcal O$ a domain that is a discrete valuation ring, $\pi\in\mathcal O$ irreducible with $\#(\mathcal O/(\pi))=r$, and $K_0$ a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. Let $g_1\in GL_2(K_0)$ be the diagonal matrix with entries $\pi,1$, let $g\in GL_2(K_0)$ be arbitrary, let $n\in\mathbb N$, and write $A_n$ for the quotient of the edge chart ring `chartERing 𝒪 π r` (the localisation away from `edgeQuot.discr 𝒪 π r`) by the ideal generated by $\pi^{n+1}$, with $\bar\xi,\bar\eta$ the images of `chartERing.ξ` and `chartERing.η`. Let $\alpha$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism of $A_n$ satisfying the transport hypothesis: for every commutative $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$, every $\mathcal O$-algebra map $xq\colon A_n\to B$ and all Deligne data $d,d'$ over $B$ for $\pi$ and $K_0$ (families of $B$-submodules $\mathrm{line}\,M\subseteq B\otimes_{\mathcal O}M$ indexed by the full $\mathcal O$-lattices $M\subset K_0^2$, with invertible quotients, monotone under inclusions, equivariant for homotheties, and nondegenerate at each prime of $B$), if $d$ has line at the standard lattice $M_0$ equal to the span of $xq(\bar\xi)\otimes e_0+1\otimes e_1$, line at $g_1M_0$ equal to the image under `actBaseChange B g₁ M₀` of the span of $1\otimes e_0+xq(\bar\eta)\otimes e_1$, and satisfies `DeligneDatum.InEdgeChart` for the pair $(g_1M_0,M_0)$ (i.e. `EdgeNondegAt` at every prime of $B$), and if $d'$ satisfies the same three conditions with $xq$ replaced by $xq\circ\alpha$, then $d'$ is the pullback of $d$ along $g^{-1}$, i.e. $d'.\mathrm{line}\,M=(d.\mathrm{line}\,(g^{-1}M))$ pulled back along `actBaseChange B g⁻¹ M` for all $M$. The conclusion is a conjunction of two implications for the action of $g$ on vertices of the tree: if $g$ fixes both the standard vertex and $g_1\cdot(\text{standard vertex})$, then $\alpha(\bar\xi)$ is associated to $\bar\xi$ and $\alpha(\bar\eta)$ to $\bar\eta$ in $A_n$; if instead $g$ interchanges these two vertices, then $\alpha(\bar\xi)$ is associated to $\bar\eta$ and $\alpha(\bar\eta)$ to $\bar\xi$.
--
--   This records how an automorphism of an edge chart ring of the formal upper half-plane, realised by transport of Deligne data along $g\in GL_2(K_0)$, acts on the two coordinates $\xi,\eta$ cutting out the vertex regions of the standard edge: the ends are preserved or exchanged according to the action of $g$ on the corresponding pair of vertices. It is used in the glueing arguments for the Mumford-style model, where pullback relations between chart data must be matched with combinatorics in the tree.
